WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.549 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  back up the yeah retro throwbacks rarely  go for the mid-nineties mix of 2d  sprites and low res 3d models but along  comes octo path traveler a game that  manages to both faithfully recreate the  aesthetic and add to it in subtle yet  meaningful ways it's a great look one  that draws you into the world and  delights you with small artistic touches  that brings something magical to  otherwise simple and familiar  environments taking pleasure in the  dreamy diorama s look of octopus will  satisfy you for a while as will the  immediately likable combat system which  implements a few small innovations to  revitalize the otherwise traditional  turn-based battle mechanics what may  ultimately trip you up however is the  narrative a collection of eight short  stories each divided up into four  chapters of increasingly higher  difficulty after picking a protagonist  at the start of the game you gather  allies by traveling to their icons on a  map this approach is viable in theory  but octa paths woefully struggles to  weave interesting tales despite the wide  range of personalities behind them  anyhow here we are coupled with wildly  varying English voice acting it's all  too easy to want to reach for the skip  button when a story sequence slowly  winds up when engaging with story  sequences everyone but the relevant  character is relegated to being backseat  companions hidden away from view  entirely the only time your team acts as  such outside of battle is during rare  opportunities that you get a banter  notification which allows for a brief  discussion between a couple of  characters depending on who you've got  in your party  these can be entertaining from time to  time but they are too infrequent and  inconsequential to truly matter it is  disappointing to report that octopus  stories are more or less a wash but that  doesn't mean the world is any less  intriguing on its own on the contrary  it's constantly refreshing to see how  much care has gone into fleshing out  run-of-the-mill NPCs many of whom have  peculiar backgrounds that outshines some  of the more mundane major characters  octopus towns are brimming with excuses  to look twice at the unsung heroes and  villains that call you rest stops home  NPCs feature other smart interactive  touches as well that call upon your  characters individual strengths just  like you'd inquire into backstories you  can steal belongings or talk strangers  into selling what you can't steal you  can allure them into following your crew  and helping out in battle or pick a  fight with them in the middle of town  just a few of your options  [Music]  but a snow day bundle  either guy without a broad objective  steering your party across the world map  your instead guided by icons that tell  you where to pick up the next chapter  for a specific character and what level  your party should be to survive the  initial stops circle a sizeable body of  water in the middle of the map with each  round of chapters shifting ever slightly  outwards towards the edge the procession  of events and markers is measured in  such a way to provide natural progress  through each character's personal  adventure keep up with the logical order  and you may never have to grind for  experience if you avoid fast traveling  to previously visited locations in order  to activate a chapter you need the  relevant character in your party but  even if you neglect to cycle party  members regularly enough to keep them on  even footing by the time they're called  upon you can still carry a grossly under  levelled character into battle without  too much concern it's one of the many  reasons why octopus battle system feels  so fresh it's about what you hit the  enemy with rather than how hard the hit  lands every enemy in octa path is  vulnerable to at least one particular  element or weapon type and most are  vulnerable to three or more you  successfully strike with a relevant  spell or weapon and icon appears so that  you've got a clear record of what to do  down the road with these tactics in mind  your goal is to break in enemy's  defenses by hitting them enough times  with effective attacks to whittle away  their shield once broken and enemy will  lose their next turn and remain in a  highly vulnerable state where attacks  hurt them a little bit more than usual  the other important piece of combat is  the battle point system battle points  act as extra swings of a weapon in a  turn or as a means to power up magical  attacks every character gets one BP add  it to their slate per turn so long as  they don't spend any which will delete  your cruel process by an additional term  in most cases saving up BP is a  beneficial way to wear down an enemies  shield in one turn with a single  character but once an enemy is broken  BP is best used to fortify single  attacks during that window of  opportunity  the concept of breaking enemies is  paramount during boss battles long  Affairs that test your ability to remain  focused on your resources characters  turn order and preparing for surprise  attacks common enemies will pose  formidable challenges as well but those  fights go a lot quicker and you're  afforded more opportunities to flex your  various skills for the fun of it rather  than to satisfy the punishing demands of  excruciating bosses your battle party is  only as good as you make them which  means not only earning enough experience  points to level them up and learn new  skills but coordinating individual skill  sets to diversify your options as a team  while also doubling down in your most  effective attacks each of the eight  characters starts with a distinct job  and as you explore you uncover shrines  to let you assign a secondary job as  well each secondary job though is  limited to one character at a time you  will no doubt come to prefer certain  jobs over others but some of the most  valuable skills are tied to characters  rather than their particular assignments  Hana the hunter for example has a unique  ability to capture enemies that can be  summoned during future battles a limited  number of times where as alpha in the  Apothecary can make medicine mid-battle  by synthesizing salves with expendable  ingredients between these unique  character traits and a variety of jobs  on-hand your party will transform on a  regular basis to keep up with the  demands of bosses and particularly  finicky enemy types this constant search  for new strategies leads to a wonderful  variety of experiences and  accomplishments by the time you reach  octopod say  [Music]  despite the lackluster stories that play  through the world Octopus thrives on its  character progression and the  temptations of high-level challenges and  rewards the promise of new jobs exciting  boss fights and powerful gear will  inspire you to poke around every corner  and there are no shortage of discoveries  to strive for and all the while you're  treated to one of the most interesting  and effective reimagining of a retro  aesthetic around octopus will likely be  a divisive game due to its fractured  storytelling but it's one worth playing  despite its trouble tales its high  points are simply too good to ignore  [Music]

